#3  The beginning of the end of the beginning
===============================================================================================

  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trapezoidal_rule
  
  We now have a discrete input signal. We want to apply a filter to it.
  We have block diagrams to help us see equations as signal paths.
  Additions and multiplications are pretty straightforward.
  But there are integrators sometimes.

  We've seen (Chapter III, #1) that integrating a function's rate of change rebuilds the function.
  But if we integrate the function itself, we get something else.
  (Antiderivative obviously, but that's not my target here.)

  /*
    In what follows I loosely use "function" to mean "function's output value".
    It's not semantically rigorous but it avoids the confusion between function output and output signal.
    I.e.: if we integrate our input signal over time, the function's input is time and its output is the input signal...
  */

  Think graphical.
  At each infinitesimal step, we multiply the function by a small increment in its variable (differential).
  On a graph, the differential is a horizontal (infinitesimal) segment; the function's value is a vertical segment.
  This figure is called a rectangle.
  And multiplying one side by the other is how we measure the area of a rectangle.

  So each step of an integral is the area of a rectangle.
  And the integration itself is the sum of all the rectangles.

  This produces an approximation of the area under the curve.
  And it's as precise as the differential is small.

  Except here our variable is time and our differential is T (the sampling period, the length of a sample).
  It's measurable and therefore not really infinitesimal.

  So the rectangle is a nice start, but it could easily benefit from some optimisation.
  
  What follows is very simple, on the sine qua non condition that you think graphical.
  Did I mention THINK GRAPHICAL?

  https://www.desmos.com/calculator/g6ozuv0whk

  Between samples, information is lost.
  But we can do better than hard-stepped jumps: the shortest way between two points is a straight line.
  And two points are all a straight line requires to be defined.

  So, knowing the previous amplitude (which we do), we would better approximate the lost curve by drawing a straight line
  to the current amplitude.

  The figure that previously was a rectangle is now a right triangle on top of a rectangle.
  (If you cannot picture it, open the last Desmos link.)
  This figure is called a TRAPEZOID.
  The method of integration we're using is called the trapezoidal rule.
  The integrator we're building is called a TRAPEZOIDAL INTEGRATOR.

  A right triangle is half a rectangle. Second easiest area to calculate: (opposite + adjacent) / 2.
  We don't need the hypotenuse. So we already have all the sides we need. Sorry Pythagoras, maybe next time.

  Let's name stuff:

    #  T is the sampling period (1 / f_s)
    #  x [n] is our current amplitude
    #  x [n - 1] is our previous amplitude

  The rectangle's horizontal side is T, its vertical side is x [n - 1].
  The triangle's adjacent and opposite sides are interchangeable depending on which angle we consider.
  Let's be arbitrary for the sake of simplicity: adjacent is T, opposite is  x [n]  -  x [n - 1].

  So the area of the rectangle is:

    T * x [n - 1]

  and the area of the right triangle is:

     T * ( x [n]  -  x [n - 1] )
    -----------------------------
                 2
  
  then the area of the trapezoid is:

                         x [n]  -  x [n - 1]         2 x [n]  -  x [n]  +  x [n - 1]       T             T
    A  =  T x [n]  +  T ---------------------  =  T ---------------------------------  =  --- x [n]  +  --- x [n - 1]
                                 2                                 2                       2             2

  If the signal has decreased,  x [n]  -  x [n - 1]  will be negative, so will the area of the triangle.
  Then the triangle will be subtracted from the rectangle. Still a trapezoid, just expressed weirdly.
  Increase works, decrease works, no third option: alles gut.

  Ok. That's the ƒ (x) dx part.
  Mathematically, it's very similar to an arithmetic mean: the one you use to calculate your average score in school.
  (Sum all items, divide the sum by the number of items.)

  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pythagorean_means

  But integration is a permanent sum.
  The area of the trapezoid (A) is what happens inside the integral, it must be added to what happened previously.

  Let's name stuff:

    #  y [n] is the current output of the integrator
    #  y [n - 1] is the previous output of the integrator

  Then:
                             T             T
    y [n]  =  y [n - 1]  +  --- x [n]  +  --- x [n - 1]
                             2             2
  
  We're almost ready to turn this into a block diagram. Except we need 1-sample delays.

  Let's say:

    x [n]  =  exp (s T n)  =  z^n

  then:

    x [n - δ]  =  exp (s T (n - δ))  =  exp (s T n  -  s T δ)  =  exp (s T n) / exp (s T δ)  =  x [n] / z^δ

  /*
    That's right, just like Laplace turns integration in division by s and differentiation in multiplication by s,
    the inverse z-transform turns a delay of n samples into a division by z^n.
    
    A multiplication by z^n would be a jump in the future.
    This is not currently technologically possible. But we can work around it using latency.
    Delay everything except one thing, and the one thing is in the future of its own referential.
    In such a case, jumps in the future would be a considerable simplification of notation, so why not?
    We won't need this digression. But anywhere else, it would have been even more off-topic.

    Due to the fact that delays are more frequent than jumps in the future, z traditionally receives negative exponents.
    I'm using 1/z for now.
    Don't be surprised if it becomes z^-1 at some point if it becomes more convenient.
  */

  Now, is y [n - 1] also a complex sinusoid?
  Re-read Chapter III if you crave insane nit picking. In short:
  Any sinusoid can be expressed using the sine function. Integrating a function's first derivative gives the initial function.
  Sine's first derivative is cosine.
  That'll do.

  Let's drop the brackets:

    x [n]      -->  x
    x [n - 1]  -->  x / z
    y [n]      -->  y
    y [n - 1]  -->  y / z

  then:                       T                 T
                             --- x       y  +  --- x
           y       T          2                 2          T
    y  =  ---  +  --- x  +  -------  =  -------------  +  --- x
           z       2           z              z            2

  Let's define:

    α  =  x T/2    ;    β  =  y + α    ;    δ  =  β / z        //  I've skipped γ because it looks like y.

  then:    y  =  α + δ

                                                                                          α
  or:        α -->(+)--> y        or:        α -->(+)--> y        or:        x -->[ T/2 ]--->(+)---> y
                   ^ +                             ^ +                                        ^ +
                   |                             δ |                                        δ |
                   δ                               |                                          |
                                                [ 1/z ]                                    [ 1/z ]
                                                   ^                                          ^
                                                   |                                        β |
                                                   β                                          |
                                                                                       α --->(+)<--- y
                                                                                           +     +
  Looks like we can close the loop:

    x -->[ T/2 ]-->o---->(+)---->o---> y                                                          
                   |      ^ +    |                  //  Note that δ needs an initial value.
                   |      |      |                  //  Some programming languages have zero by default.
                   |   [ 1/z ]   |                  //  (EEL2 / JSFX for instance)
                   |      ^      |                  //  Most will require an explicit δ = 0 during initialisation.
                   |      |      |                  //  (Of course anything will do. Zero is safe.
                   ----->(+)<-----                  //  But where's the fun in safe?)
                       +     +
  
  This is a trapezoidal integrator. Probably not the last one you'll see.
  I suck at small talk. But I'm trying to force a pause here.
  Because this is fundamental.

  There are basically two schools of digital filter design. More on that later.
  This humble piece of block diagram is the core element of one of them.
  (It's notably on the front page of Vadim Zavalishin's "The Art of V.A. Filter Design".)

  It comes in (at least?) 4 different forms. This is Transposed Direct Form II (TDF2).

  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_biquad_filter#Transposed_direct_form_2

  The name suggests that it's the less straightforward form...
  I must be wired in opposite polarity because the formula always leads me to this form.
  I easily get to Direct Form I (DF1) by not simplifying the equation at all (but why would you do that?).
  But Direct Form II (DF2) and Transposed Direct Form I (DF1) are mysteries to me: show me the solution, I get the math.
  But how to seek this solution in the first place? Either I'm twisted or everyone else is.

  So I'll stick to TDF2 because for knowledge to be shared, it has to be knowledge.
  Look at Wikipedia and make your own choice. DF1 and DF2 have two [ 1/z ] elements. TDF1 and TDF2 factorise them out.

  We could already replace [ 1/s ] elements in our block diagrams and obtain digital filters. But I've already done the end credits joke.

  Trapezoidal integrator has an input. Trapezoidal integrator has an output. Trapezoidal integrator has a transfer function!

                   α
    x -->[ T/2 ]-->o---->(+)---->o---> y        //    y  =  α + δ    ;    δ  =  β / z    ;    β  =  α + y    ;    α  =  x T/2
                   |      ^ +    |              //    
                   |    δ |      |              //    β / z  =  y - α    <=>    (α + y) / z  =  y - α    <=>    α/z + y/z  =  y - α
                   |      |      |              //
                   |   [ 1/z ]   |              //    <=>    α/z + α  =  y - y/z    <=>    y (1 - z^-1)  =  α (z^-1 + 1)
                   |      ^      |              //
                   |    β |      |              //                    1 + z^-1         T     1 + z^-1                       T     1 + z^-1
                   |      |      |              //    <=>    y  =  α ----------  =  x --- * ----------    <=>    H (z)  =  --- * ----------
