  //  Trapezoidal differentiator

  This piece of block diagram can be injected in bigger block diagrams by replacing [ 1/s ] elements. Fine.
  But what if we want to directly discretise a filter's transfer function? We need to replace s.

  Differentiation is the reciprocal to integration.
  So we need the reciprocal to a trapezoidal integrator.
  A function maps x to y. Its reciprocal maps y to x.
  So we need to treat x as the unknown and express it in terms of y.

  I chose this method because I find it easier. But reading the block diagram backwards is as valid a solution.

              T     1 + z^-1                     T     1 + z^-1             2         1 + z^-1                     2     1 - z^-1
    H (z)  = --- * ----------    <=>    y  =  x --- * ----------  <=>    y ---  =  x ----------    <=>    x  =  y --- * ----------
              2     1 - z^-1                     2     1 - z^-1             T         1 - z^-1                     T     1 + z^-1

  Ok, so the reciprocal is the inverse. Laplace's magic is still operating. Good.

  Then, if H_a (s) is an analog filter's transfer function, and H_d (z) is its trapezoidal discretised counterpart,
  the substitution:    H_a (s)  <--  H_d (z)    requires the substitution:

             2     1 - z^-1
    s  <--  --- * ----------
             T     1 + z^-1

